              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>Data_byte_7</name>
              <description>Received payload corresponding to data byte 7 under Pretended Networking mode.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>


        <register>
        <name>EPRS</name>
          <description>The CAN_EPRS register defines the CAN bit timing prescalers for the nominal phase and data phase when CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set.
CAN_EPRS is used by the hardware only if CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set; otherwise a write operation has no effect and all fields are read as zero.
CAN_EPRS can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.
The contents of CAN_EPRS are not affected by soft reset.
The CAN_EPRS register is not available when parameter CIA_BIT_TIMING is 0.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xbf0</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EDPRESDIV</name>
              <description>EDPRESDIV defines the ratio between the PE clock frequency and the Serial Clock (Sclock) frequency in the data bit rate portion of a CAN FD message when CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set.
The Sclock period defines the time quantum of the CAN FD protocol for the data bit rate.
Sclock frequency = PE clock frequency / (EDPRESDIV + 1).
NOTE: To minimize errors when processing FD frames, use the same value for ENPRESDIV and EDPRESDIV.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ENPRESDIV</name>
              <description>ENPRESDIV defines the ratio between the PE clock frequency and the Serial Clock (Sclock) frequency for the nominal bit rate when CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set.
The Sclock period defines the time quantum of the CAN protocol for the nominal phase.
Sclock frequency = PE clock frequency / (ENPRESDIV + 1).</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>ENCBT</name>
          <description>The CAN_ENCBT register provides an alternative way to store the CAN bit timing variables described in the CAN_CTRL1 and CAN_CBT registers, to achieve higher CAN bit timing resolution.
CAN_ENCBT is used by the hardware only if CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set; otherwise a write operation has no effect and all fields are read as zero.
CAN_ENCBT can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.
The contents of CAN_ENCBT are not affected by soft reset.
The CAN_ENCBT register is not available when parameter CIA_BIT_TIMING is 0.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xbf4</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>NRJW</name>
              <description>NRJW defines the maximum number of time quanta that a nominal bit time can be changed by one resynchronization when CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1.
When CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 0, NRJW has no effect. One time quantum is equal to one Sclock period.
Nominal Resynchronization Jump Width = NRJW + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NTSEG2</name>
              <description>NTSEG2 defines the length of Time Segment 2 in the nominal bit time when CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1.
When CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 0, NTSEG2 has no effect.
Nominal Time Segment 2 = (NTSEG2 + 1) × Time-Quanta
Time-Quantum = one Sclock period</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NTSEG1</name>
              <description>NTSEG1 defines the length of Time Segment 1 in the nominal bit time when CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1.
When CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 0, NTSEG1 has no effect.
Nominal Time Segment 1 = (NTSEG1 + 1) × Time-Quanta
Time-Quantum = one Sclock period</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>EDCBT</name>
          <description>The CAN_EDCBT register provides an alternative way to store the data phase CAN bit timing variables described in the CAN_FDCBT register, to achieve higher CAN bit timing resolution.
CAN_EDCBT is used by the hardware only if CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set; otherwise a write operation has no effect and all fields are read as zero.
CAN_EDCBT can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.
The contents of CAN_EDCBT are not affected by soft reset.
The CAN_EDCBT register is not available when parameter CIA_BIT_TIMING is 0.
NOTE: CAN_EDCBT.DTSEG1 must be at least two time quanta.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xbf8</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DRJW</name>
              <description>DRJW defines the maximum number of time quanta that a data phase bit time can be changed by one resynchronization when CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1.
When CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 0, DRJW has no effect.
One time quantum is equal to one Sclock period.
Data Phase Resynchronization Jump Width = DRJW + 1.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DTSEG2</name>
              <description>DTSEG2 defines the length of Time Segment 2 in the data phase bit time when CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1.
When CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 0, DTSEG2 has no effect.
Data Phase Time Segment 2 = (DTSEG2 + 1) × Time-Quanta
Time-Quantum = one Sclock period</description>
              <bitOffset>12</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>4</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DTSEG1</name>
              <description>DTSEG1 defines the length of Time Segment 1 in the data phase bit time when CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1.
When CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 0, DTSEG1 has no effect.
Data Phase Time Segment 1 = (DTSEG1 + 1) × Time-Quanta
Time-Quantum = one Sclock period</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>ETDC</name>
          <description>The CAN_ETDC register contains extended versions of the fields CAN_FDCTRL.TDCOFF and CAN_FDCTRL.TDCVAL.
CAN_ETDC is used by the hardware only if CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set; otherwise a write operation has no effect and all fields are read as zero.
The CAN_ETDC register is not available when parameter CIA_BIT_TIMING is 0.
NOTE: See the transmitter delay compensation section of the CAN Protocol Standard (ISO 11898-1) for additional details.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xbfc</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ETDCEN</name>
              <description>ETDCEN can be written in Freeze mode only.
NOTE: TDC must be disabled when Loop-Back mode is enabled (see CAN_CTRL1.LPB).</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>TDC is disabled.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>TDC is enabled.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TDMDIS</name>
              <description>When TDC measurement is disabled, the secondary sample point position is determined only by the enhanced TDC offset (ETDCOFF) field. Otherwise, if TCD measurement is enabled, then the secondary sample point position is determined by the sum of the transceiver delay measurement plus the enhanced TDC offset.
TDMDIS is not affected by soft reset.
TDMDIS can be enabled only if CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>TDC measurement is enabled.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>TDC measurement is disabled.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ETDCOFF</name>
              <description>ETDCOFF contains the offset value to be added to the measured transceiver’s loop delay in order to define the position of the delayed comparison point when bit rate switching is active. See “Transceiver Delay Compensation” on page 187 for additional details on how the loop delay measurement is performed.
ETDCOFF can be written in Freeze mode only. Its value is defined in protocol engine (PE) clock periods and must be selected to be smaller than the CAN bit duration in the data phase bit rate for proper operation.
ETDCOFF must not be configured as zero.
NOTE: If CAN_CTRL2.BTE is set after a chip-level hard reset, ETCDOFF is read as 0x1.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ETDCFAIL</name>
              <description>ETDCFAIL indicates when the Transceiver Delay Compensation (TDC) mechanism is out of range, unable to compensate the transceiver's loop delay and successfully compare the delayed received bits to the transmitted ones
ETDCFAIL is set the first time the FlexCAN detects the out of range condition. To clear ETDCFAIL, write 1 to it. See \hyperref[transceiver-delay-compensation]{"Transceiver Delay Compensation"}.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Measured loop delay is in range.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Measured loop delay is out of range.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ETDCVAL</name>
              <description>ETDCVAL contains the ETDCOFF field added to the measured value of the transceiver loop delay in the latest transmitted CAN FD frame with BRS equal to recessive.
ETDCVAL is only updated by the hardware if the ETDCEN bit is set.
ETDCVAL is affected by soft reset.
NOTE: If TDMDIS is set, ETDCVAL stores ETDCOFF only.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>8</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>FDCTRL</name>
          <description></description>
          <addressOffset>0xc00</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FDRATE</name>
              <description>The CPU can write the FDRATE bit any time. However, its effect becomes active only when the CAN bus is in the Wait for Bus Idle, Bus Idle, or Bus Off state, or when the current frame under reception or transmission reaches the interframe space.
By negating FDRATE, the CPU can force all bits in CAN FD messages to be transmitted in nominal bit rate, regardless of the value of the BRS bit of the Tx MBs.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Transmit a frame in nominal rate. The BRS bit in the Tx MB has no effect.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Transmit a frame with bit rate switching if the BRS bit in the Tx MB is recessive.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MBDSR3</name>
              <description>For region R3 of message buffers allocated in RAM:
  • 00: Selects 8 bytes per message buffer.
  • 01: Selects 16 bytes per message buffer.
  • 10: Selects 32 bytes per message buffer.
  • 11: Selects 64 bytes per message buffer.
MBDSR3 can be written in Freeze mode only.</description>
              <bitOffset>25</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MBDSR2</name>
              <description>For region R2 of message buffers allocated in RAM:
  • 00: Selects 8 bytes per message buffer.
  • 01: Selects 16 bytes per message buffer.
  • 10: Selects 32 bytes per message buffer.
  • 11: Selects 64 bytes per message buffer.
MBDSR2 can be written in Freeze mode only.</description>
              <bitOffset>22</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MBDSR1</name>
              <description>For region R1 of message buffers allocated in RAM:
  • 00: Selects 8 bytes per message buffer.
  • 01: Selects 16 bytes per message buffer.
  • 10: Selects 32 bytes per message buffer.
  • 11: Selects 64 bytes per message buffer.
MBDSR1 can be written in Freeze mode only.</description>
              <bitOffset>19</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MBDSR0</name>
              <description>For region R0 of message buffers allocated in RAM:
  • 00: Selects 8 bytes per message buffer.
  • 01: Selects 16 bytes per message buffer.
  • 10: Selects 32 bytes per message buffer.
  • 11: Selects 64 bytes per message buffer.
MBDSR0 can be written in Freeze mode only.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>2</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TDCEN</name>
              <description>TDCEN can be written in Freeze mode only.
NOTE: TDC must be disabled when Loop-Back mode is enabled (see CAN_CTRL1.LPB).
NOTE: If CAN_CTRL2.BTE =1, TDCEN is read as zero and a write operation has no effect.</description>
              <bitOffset>15</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>TDC is disabled.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>TDC is enabled.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TDCFAIL</name>
              <description>TDCFAIL indicates when the Transceiver Delay Compensation (TDC) mechanism is out of range, unable to compensate the transceiver’s loop delay, and successfully compare the delayed received bits to the transmitted ones.

TDCFAIL is set the first time the FlexCAN detects the out of range condition. To clear TDCFAIL, write 1 to it.

See \hyperref[transceiver-delay-compensation]{Transceiver Delay Compensation}.

NOTE: If CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1, TDCFAIL is read as zero and a write operation has no effect.</description>
              <bitOffset>14</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Measured loop delay is in range</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Measured loop delay is out of range</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TDCOFF</name>
              <description>TDCOFF contains the offset value to be added to the measured transceiver’s loop delay in order to define the position of the delayed comparison point when bit rate switching is active. See \hyperref[transceiver-delay-compensation]{Transceiver Delay Compensation} for more details on how the loop delay measurement is performed.
TDCOFF can be written in Freeze mode only. Its value can be defined in PE clock periods and must be selected to be smaller than the CAN bit duration in the data bit rate for proper operation.
NOTE: It is not recommended to use TDCOFF equal to zero.
NOTE: If CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1, TDCOFF is read as zero and a write operation has no effect.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>TDCVAL</name>
              <description>TDCVAL contains the value of the transceiver loop delay measured from the transmitted EDL to R0 transition edge to the respective received one added to the TDCOFF value.
This value is an integer multiple of the PE clock period.
See \hyperref[protocol-timing]{"Protocol Timing"} for more details on how the loop delay measurement is performed.
NOTE: If CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1, TDCVAL is read as zero.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>FDCBT</name>
          <description>The CAN_FDCBT register stores the CAN bit timing variables used in the data phase of CAN FD messages when CAN_FDCTRL.FDRATE is set, compatible with CAN FD specification. FPRESDIV, FPROPSEG, FPSEG1, FPSEG2, and FRJW are used to define the time quantum duration, the number of time quanta per CAN bit, and the sample point position for the data bit rate portion of a CAN FD message with the BRS bit set.

The contents of CAN_FDCBT are not affected by soft reset.

NOTE: The sum of the Fast Propagation Segment (FPROPSEG) and Fast Phase Segment 1 (FPSEG1) must be at least two time quanta.

NOTE: If CAN_CTRL2.BTE = 1, the CAN_FDCBT register is read as zero and a write operation has no effect.

The CAN_FDCBT register is not available when parameter FD_EN is 0.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc04</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FPRESDIV</name>
              <description>FPRESDIV defines the ratio between the PE clock frequency and the Serial Clock (Sclock) frequency in the data bit rate portion of a CAN FD message with the BRS bit set.
The Sclock period defines the time quantum of the CAN FD protocol for the data bit rate.
Sclock frequency = PE clock frequency / (FPRESDIV + 1).
NOTE: To minimize errors when processing FD frames, use the same value for FPRESDIV and PRESDIV (in CAN_CBT or CAN_CTRL1). For more details, see \hyperref[can-fd-frames]{"CAN FD Frames"}.
FPRESDIV can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes</description>
              <bitOffset>20</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>10</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FRJW</name>
              <description>FRJW defines the maximum number of time quanta that a bit time can be changed by one re-synchronization in the data bit rate portion of a CAN FD message with the BRS bit set.
Resync Jump Width = FSJW + 1.
One time quantum is equal to the Sclock period.
FRJW can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FPROPSEG</name>
              <description>FPROPSEG defines the length of the Propagation Segment in the bit time in the data bit rate portion of a CAN FD message with the BRS bit set.
Propagation Segment Time = FPROPSEG × Time-Quanta
Time-Quantum = one Sclock period
FPROPSEG can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.</description>
              <bitOffset>10</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FPSEG1</name>
              <description>FPSEG1 defines the length of Phase Segment 1 in the bit time in the data bit rate portion of a CAN FD message with the BRS bit set.
Phase Segment 1 = (FPSEG1 + 1) × Time-Quanta
Time-Quantum = one Sclock period
FPSEG1 can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.</description>
              <bitOffset>5</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FPSEG2</name>
              <description>FPSEG2 defines the length of Phase Segment 2 in the data bit rate portion of a CAN FD message with the BRS bit set.
Phase Segment 2 = (FPSEG2 + 1) × Time-Quanta
Time-Quantum = one Sclock period
FPSEG2 can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>3</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>FDCRC</name>
          <description></description>
          <addressOffset>0xc08</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FD_MBCRC</name>
              <description>FD_MBCRC indicates the number of the mailbox corresponding to the value in the FD_TXCRC field, for both FD and non-FD frames.
FD_MBCRC reports the same information as in the CAN_CRCR.MBCRC bit field.</description>
              <bitOffset>24</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>FD_TXCRC</name>
              <description>FD_TXCRC contains the CRC value calculated over the most recent transmitted message. Different CRC polynomials are used for different frame formats. 
A 15-bit polynomial, CRC_15, is used for all frames in CAN format. 
The second 17-bit polynomial, CRC_17, is used for frames in CAN FD format with a data field up to sixteen bytes long. 
The third 21-bit polynomial, CRC_21, is used for frames in CAN FD format with a data field longer than sixteen bytes.
For CRC_15 and CRC_17, the 6 most significant bits and the 4 most significant bits are reported as zeros, respectively. 
For CRC_15, this register has the same content as CAN_CRCR.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>21</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>ERFCR</name>
          <description>The CAN_ERFCR register is not available when parameter ERX_FIFO_EN is 0.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc0c</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ERFEN</name>
              <description>ERFEN can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.
NOTE: ERFEN must not be set if CAN_MCR.RFEN is set.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Disable Enhanced Rx FIFO.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Enable Enhanced Rx FIFO.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DMALW</name>
              <description>Table \hyperref[CAN-DMALW-value-and-number-of-enhanced-RX-FIFO-elements-transferred]{"CAN DMALW value and number of enhanced RX FIFO elements transferred"} shows the number of elements and the last address for each Enhanced Rx FIFO element according to the value of DMALW.
DMALW values not shown in Table \hyperref[CAN-DMALW-value-and-number-of-enhanced-RX-FIFO-elements-transferred]{"CAN DMALW value and number of enhanced RX FIFO elements transferred"} are reserved and must not be used.
DMALW can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.</description>
              <bitOffset>26</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NEXIF</name>
              <description>NEXIF must be less than or equal to NFE + 1.
The number of standard ID filter elements can be determined by the following equation:
Number of Standard ID Filter Elements = 2 x (NFE - NEXIF +1)
\hyperref[CAN-NEXIF-value-and-number-of-extended-or-standard-ID-filter-elements]{"CAN NEXIF value and number of extended or standard ID filter elements"} shows the number of extended ID filters and standard ID filters available for Enhanced Rx FIFO if all the filter elements are used.
NEXIF can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.</description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>7</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>NFE</name>
              <description>NFE can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.</description>
              <bitOffset>8</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFWM</name>
              <description>Minimum number of CAN messages = ERFWM + 1.
When ERFWM + 1 is reached, ERFSR.ERFWMI is set.
ERFWM can be written only in Freeze mode because it is blocked by hardware in other modes.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>5</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>ERFIER</name>
          <description>The CAN_ERFIER register is not available when parameter ERX_FIFO_EN is 0.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc10</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ERFUFWIE</name>
              <description></description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Disable Enhanced Rx FIFO underflow interrupt.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Enable Enhanced Rx FIFO underflow interrupt.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFOVFIE</name>
              <description></description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Disable Enhanced Rx FIFO overflow interrupt.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Enable Enhanced Rx FIFO overflow interrupt.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFWMIIE</name>
              <description></description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Disable Enhanced Rx FIFO watermark interrupt.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Enable Enhanced Rx FIFO watermark interrupt.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFDAIE</name>
              <description></description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Disable Enhanced Rx FIFO data available interrupt.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Enable Enhanced Rx FIFO data available interrupt.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>ERFSR</name>
          <description>The CAN_ERFSR register is not available when parameter ERX_FIFO_EN is 0.</description>
          <addressOffset>0xc14</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ERFUFW</name>
              <description>ERFUFW is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>31</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Enhanced Rx FIFO underflow has not occurred.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Enhanced Rx FIFO underflow has occurred. Interrupt ipi_int_erfufw is asserted if CAN_ERFIER.ERFUFWIE = 1.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFOVF</name>
              <description>ERFOVF is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>30</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>Enhanced Rx FIFO overflow has not occurred.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>Enhanced Rx FIFO overflow has occurred. Interrupt ipi_int_erfovf is asserted if CAN_ERFIER.ERFOVFIE = 1.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFWMI</name>
              <description>ERFWMI is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>29</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>The number of messages in the Enhanced Rx FIFO is not greater than the watermark.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>The number of messages in the Enhanced Rx FIFO is greater than the watermark. Interrupt ipi_int_erfwm is asserted if CAN_ERFIER.ERFWMIIE = 1.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFDA</name>
              <description>ERFDA is cleared by writing 1 to it.</description>
              <bitOffset>28</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>There is no message stored in the Enhanced Rx FIFO.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>There is at least one message stored in the Enhanced Rx FIFO. Interrupt ipi_int_erfda is asserted if CAN_ERFIER.ERFDAIE = 1.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFCLR</name>
              <description>Writing 1 to ERFCLR when the FlexCAN is in Freeze mode clears the Enhanced Rx FIFO content.
Writing 0 to ERFCLR or writing to ERFCLR when the FlexCAN is not in Freeze mode has no effect.</description>
              <bitOffset>27</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFE</name>
              <description></description>
              <bitOffset>17</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>The Enhanced Rx FIFO is not empty.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>The Enhanced Rx FIFO is empty.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFF</name>
              <description></description>
              <bitOffset>16</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>1</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>0</name>
                  <description>The Enhanced Rx FIFO is not full.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>1</name>
                  <description>The Enhanced Rx FIFO is full.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>ERFEL</name>
              <description></description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>6</bitWidth>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>HR_TIME_STAMPn0</name>
          <description></description>
          <addressOffset>0xc30</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TS</name>
              <description>Capture of 32-bit timer value for the corresponding message buffer. The capture point depends on the value of CAN_CTRL2.TSTAMPCAP, as shown in \hyperref[CAN-high-resolution-time-stamp-register-operation]{"CAN high resolution time stamp register operation"}.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>HR_TIME_STAMPn1</name>
          <description></description>
          <addressOffset>0xc34</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
              <name>TS</name>
              <description>Capture of 32-bit timer value for the corresponding message buffer. The capture point depends on the value of CAN_CTRL2.TSTAMPCAP, as shown in \hyperref[CAN-high-resolution-time-stamp-register-operation]{"CAN high resolution time stamp register operation"}.</description>
              <bitOffset>0</bitOffset>
              <bitWidth>32</bitWidth>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>

        <register>
        <name>HR_TIME_STAMPn2</name>
          <description></description>
          <addressOffset>0xc38</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x00000000</resetValue>
           <fields>
            <field>
